//! lotus/stereo_silence_span.rye — ALES122: stereo_silence_span — silencing a span carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. ALES110 named `silence_span` — write silence over the
//! `count` samples starting at `start` IN PLACE, so [start, start+count) becomes zero and the length HOLDS — for a mono Clip. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard
//! together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME length so the two channels stay aligned in time. This rung carries silence_span into stereo the way ALES121
//! carried paste_over: run ALES110's proven mono `silence_span` on each channel with the SAME `start` and `count`, so muting a bleed or dropping a cough clears both speakers at once.
//! It is exactly the zero-paint case of ALES121's stereo_paste_over — silence is the overwrite whose paint is zero — and the length-preserving third beside stereo_cut (which SHRINKS).
//!
//! The rule, stated once: stereo_silence_span(sc, start, count) writes silence over [start, start+count) in both channels, reusing ALES110's mono `silence_span` per channel over its
//! public API. BOTH channels are validated BEFORE either is mutated — because mono silence_span can fault only with BadRange and both channels enter equal-length, the span is checked
//! once against the shared length up front, so once it passes each mono silence_span is pre-validated to succeed and a refusal never desynchronises the channels. It reuses ALES2's
//! EditError whole and invents none of its own.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO SILENCE LAW: the left channel equals mono silence_span(left, start, count) and the right equals mono silence_span(right, start, count), each byte for
//! byte, the span all zero and every sample outside it unchanged. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length — silencing writes
//! values only, never resizing, so the stereo image stays aligned. THE SPECIALIZATION LAW: stereo_silence_span(start, count) equals ALES121's stereo_paste_over(start, zeros, zeros) byte
//! for byte on both channels — silence is the overwrite whose paint is zero. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced;
//! count 0 the identity on both, silence_span(0, len) silences both wholly, a span past the samples BadRange.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES122.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-021904_fill-ales122-lotus-stereo-silence-span.md
//! Stands on: lotus/silence_span.rye (ALES110) — the mono span-silence run once per channel; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance
//! invariant; lotus/stereo_paste_over.rye (ALES121) — the stereo overwrite this rung specializes (zero paint), imported by the selftest to prove the specialization law; lotus/timeline.rye
//! (ALES2) — the Clip and the EditError reused whole; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) — the peak/RMS meter, used only by the witness to read the result.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It writes zeros over an existing span in each channel —
//! changing no length and reading no byte past either channel's len. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_silence_span.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const silence_span = @import("silence_span.rye");
const stereo_paste_over = @import("stereo_paste_over.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");

pub const stereo_silence_span_version = "20260815.021904";

/// stereo_silence_span forwards ALES2's OWN fault reused by name — BadRange when the span falls outside the current samples — and invents none of its own.
pub const StereoSilenceSpanError = timeline.EditError;

/// Write silence over [start, start+count) in both channels in lockstep — the stereo image staying balanced and the length holding. Both channels are validated before either is
/// mutated: because mono silence_span can fault only with BadRange and the channels enter equal-length, the span is checked once against the shared length up front, so a refusal never
/// leaves one channel silenced and the other not. Reuses ALES110's mono silence_span per channel.
pub fn stereo_silence_span(sc: *pan.StereoClip, start: u32, count: u32) StereoSilenceSpanError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the (shared) current samples, checked without underflow — count against the remainder — refused by name before any write.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono silence_span is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits.
    try silence_span.silence_span(&sc.left, start, count);
    try silence_span.silence_span(&sc.right, start, count);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — silencing writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO SILENCE LAW — each channel equals ALES110's mono silence_span on that channel byte for byte, the length held; THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — left.len == right.len
//     after, both holding their starting length; THE SPECIALIZATION LAW — stereo_silence_span equals ALES121's stereo_paste_over with zero payloads; THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW — a
//     refusal leaves both channels untouched, count 0 the identity, silence_span(0, len) silences both wholly, a span past the samples BadRange ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO SILENCE LAW — each channel equals ALES110's mono silence_span on that channel, proven side by side over one anchor with different per-channel samples.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 }, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        try stereo_silence_span(&sc, 1, 2); // silence [1,3) in both channels

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        try silence_span.silence_span(&ml, 1, 2);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        try silence_span.silence_span(&mr, 1, 2);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // Concretely: {10,0,0,40,50} on the left, {11,0,0,41,51} on the right; length held at 5.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 0, 0, 40, 50 })) return error.LeftSpanWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 11, 0, 0, 41, 51 })) return error.RightSpanWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; the stereo image stays aligned. Proven at the head and through the tail.
    {
        var head = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&head, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8 });
        try stereo_silence_span(&head, 0, 1); // silence the head
        if (head.left.len != head.right.len) return error.HeadUnbalanced;
        if (head.left.len != 3) return error.HeadLength;
        if (!eq(head.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 2, 3 })) return error.HeadLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(head.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 7, 8 })) return error.HeadRightWrong;

        var tail = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&tail, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8 });
        try stereo_silence_span(&tail, 1, 2); // through the tail
        if (tail.left.len != tail.right.len) return error.TailUnbalanced;
        if (!eq(tail.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 0, 0 })) return error.TailLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(tail.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 6, 0, 0 })) return error.TailRightWrong;
    }

    // 3. THE SPECIALIZATION LAW — stereo_silence_span(start, count) equals ALES121's stereo_paste_over(start, zeros, zeros) byte for byte on both channels: silence is the overwrite
    //    whose paint is zero.
    {
        const l0 = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 };
        const r0 = [_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 };

        var silenced = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&silenced, &l0, &r0);
        try stereo_silence_span(&silenced, 1, 2);

        var pasted = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&pasted, &l0, &r0);
        try stereo_paste_over.stereo_paste_over(&pasted, 1, &[_]i16{ 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0 });

        if (!eq(silenced.left.samples(), pasted.left.samples())) return error.SpecializationLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(silenced.right.samples(), pasted.right.samples())) return error.SpecializationRightWrong;
    }

    // 4a. THE ATOMICITY LAW — a refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced, proven for a start past the samples and a count past the remainder.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 });

        if (stereo_silence_span(&sc, 4, 1)) |_| return error.StartNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (stereo_silence_span(&sc, 2, 5)) |_| return error.CountNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.RefusedWroteLeft;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 })) return error.RefusedWroteRight;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.RefusedUnbalanced;
    }

    // 4b. THE DEGENERATE LAW — count 0 the identity on both channels, and silence_span(0, len) silences both wholly.
    {
        var zero = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&zero, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        try stereo_silence_span(&zero, 1, 0); // silence nothing — the identity
        if (!eq(zero.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 })) return error.ZeroLeftMoved;
        if (!eq(zero.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.ZeroRightMoved;

        var whole = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&whole, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        try stereo_silence_span(&whole, 0, 3); // silence the whole span
        if (!eq(whole.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 })) return error.WholeLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(whole.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 })) return error.WholeRightWrong;
    }

    _ = meter;

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-silence-span: STEREO_SILENCE_SPAN — silencing a span carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. Write silence over [start, start+count) in both " ++
        "channels, reusing ALES110's mono silence_span per channel; a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length, so " ++
        "muting a bleed clears both speakers at once. It is exactly the zero-paint case of ALES121's stereo_paste_over — silence is the overwrite whose paint is zero — and the " ++
        "length-preserving third beside stereo_cut (which shrinks). BOTH channels are validated before either is mutated — because mono silence_span can fault only with BadRange and " ++
        "the channels enter equal-length, the span is checked once against the shared length up front, so a refusal never desynchronises the channels. The crux is four laws. THE " ++
        "STEREO SILENCE LAW: each channel equals ALES110's mono silence_span on that channel byte for byte, the span all zero and every sample outside it unchanged. THE BALANCE / " ++
        "LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length. THE SPECIALIZATION LAW: stereo_silence_span equals ALES121's stereo_paste_over with zero " ++
        "payloads byte for byte on both channels. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal leaves both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced, count 0 the identity on " ++
        "both, silence_span(0, len) silencing both wholly, a span past the samples BadRange. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips silenced through the proven mono silence_span, reading " ++
        "no byte past either channel's len; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
